{"purpose":"Record that the irreversible MCP tool lacks the confirmation gate its reversible sibling enforces, an inverted safety gradient, and state the acceptance criteria.","content":"## 1. Observed asymmetry\n`transition` is gated: a risky move returns a refusal carrying a conformance report plus an explicit `retry with confirmed:true` affordance, and the write happens only on the confirmed retry.\n\n`discard` is not gated. It takes two parameters (`target`, `project`), writes unconditionally, and its description says the draft is gone and the identity is free for a new draft:\n\n```go\n// server.go\nName:        \"discard\",\nRiskClass:   RiskLocalWrite,\nRequired:    []string{\"target\"},\nProperties:  // target, project — no confirmed field\n```\n\n## 2. Why the gradient is inverted\n`transition` is recoverable: a work item can move forward again, and a plan can be revisited. `discard` destroys the draft file and is not recoverable through any tool. The gate therefore protects the reversible operation and leaves the irreversible one exposed.\n\n## 3. Additional consideration\nThe discard identity becomes immediately reusable after deletion, so an agent that discards and re-scaffolds the same identity can silently replace authored content. A confirmation gate makes that sequence deliberate rather than accidental.\n\n## 4. Acceptance criteria\n- `discard` refuses without an explicit confirmation parameter, returning the same style of actionable refusal the transition path returns.\n- The confirmation parameter is documented in the tool schema so an agent can discover it without trial and error.\n- A test asserts that an unconfirmed discard performs no deletion and that the confirmed retry deletes exactly once.\n- The tool's destructive nature is advertised in its machine-readable annotations, so clients that surface approval prompts can do so."}